## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design scalable data lifecycle with multi-tenant governance and cost control; integrates Iceberg features, storage tiering, policy-driven retention, and query-layer masking.

## Key Concepts
- Iceberg time travel via snapshots
- Dynamic data tiering (hot/warm/cold)
- Per-tenant data masking
- Encryption at rest (KMS)
- Partition pruning and metadata caching

## Code Example
```javascript
function selectTier(tenant, ageDays) {
  const retention = (tenantRetention[tenant] || 365);
  if (ageDays < 30) return 'hot';
  if (ageDays < retention) return 'warm';
  return 'cold';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test data freshness and mask correctness across tiers?
- How would you validate time travel remains accurate after tiering moves?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to blend streaming and batch processing with a practical ML feature store, including contracts, versioning, lineage, and schema evolution—common in large tech orgs.

## Key Concepts
- Lakehouse (Iceberg) with feature tables
- Versioned feature registry and per-record data contracts
- Real-time (Flink) vs batch (Spark) feature computation
- Idempotent upserts and late-data handling
- End-to-end data lineage and monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
function validateRecord(record, schema) {
  // naive contract: required keys and types
  for (const [k, t] of Object.entries(schema.fields)) {
    if (!(k in record)) return { ok: false, error: `missing ${k}` };
    if (typeof record[k] !== t) return { ok: false, error: `${k} type` };
  }
  return { ok: true };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you isolate feature namespaces for different teams and enforce RBAC?
- How would you test schema evolution with backward-compat migrations?
